WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.707
We learned how to scan, sort, and join.

00:00:05.707 --> 00:00:09.415
Now the question that changes everything.

00:00:09.415 --> 00:00:13.123
Can we skip the scan entirely?

00:00:13.123 --> 00:00:16.830
Yes, if you build an index.

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:20.538
Without one, finding Mickey's listens means reading every row in a billion-row table.

00:00:20.538 --> 00:00:24.245
With one, you ask the index where Mickey lives, and go straight there.

00:00:24.245 --> 00:00:28.709
So what is an index?

00:00:28.709 --> 00:00:33.173
Look at the same information stored two ways.

00:00:33.173 --> 00:00:37.637
The Songs table holds the rows themselves.

00:00:37.637 --> 00:00:42.101
Whole songs, title, artist, audio, ten megabytes each.

00:00:42.101 --> 00:00:46.565
A million of them is ten terabytes, about one hundred sixty thousand pages on disk.

00:00:46.565 --> 00:00:51.029
The index holds only key plus page number pointers.

00:00:51.029 --> 00:00:55.493
Beatles, page forty-seven.

00:00:55.493 --> 00:00:59.957
Queen, page twenty-three.

00:00:59.957 --> 00:01:04.422
Eight to twenty bytes apiece, so a million of them fit in twenty megabytes, a single page.

00:01:04.422 --> 00:01:08.886
Same logical information, five hundred thousand times smaller, because a pointer is tiny next to the row it points at.

00:01:08.886 --> 00:01:13.350
Put them on the same horizontal scale and the index is barely visible.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:17.814
That gap is the whole point.

00:01:17.814 --> 00:01:22.278
The query WHERE artist equals Beatles without an index scans all one hundred sixty thousand pages.

00:01:22.278 --> 00:01:26.742
With one, the database reeds a single index page to learn Beatles lives on page forty-seven, then reeds page forty-seven.

00:01:26.742 --> 00:01:31.206
Two I Os instead of one hundred sixty thousand.

00:01:31.206 --> 00:01:35.670
Eighty thousand times fewer reeds for the same answer, and the win grows with table size.

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:43.646
An index is a tradeoff.

00:01:43.646 --> 00:01:51.622
Faster reeds in exchange for extra storage and slower writes.

00:01:51.622 --> 00:01:59.598
Add one on primary keys, on foreign keys that joins walk constantly, on high-selectivity columns like email or user id, and on columns your WHERE clauses target.

00:01:59.598 --> 00:02:07.574
Skip it on rarely queried columns, on low-selectivity columns like a boolean where the index points at half the table, on columns that change on every write, and on small tables where a scan is already fast.

00:02:07.574 --> 00:02:12.613
Indexes store key plus page number pointers, not data.

00:02:12.613 --> 00:02:17.651
That is why they are five hundred thousand times smaller than the table they index, and why two I Os beats one hundred sixty thousand.

00:02:17.651 --> 00:02:22.690
Every index type in this module is a different way of organizing those same pairs.

00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:27.729
Hash for equality, B-plus trees for ranges, SS Tables for immutable files, LSM trees for write-heavy stores.

00:02:27.729 --> 00:02:32.767
Next, the simplest one.

00:02:32.767 --> 00:02:37.806
Hash.
